WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:05.512
Trigonometric Functions. What each parameter
does to the graph.

00:07.712 --> 00:11.366
This is the function f(x) = a + b·sin(c·x +
d).

00:11.416 --> 00:14.361
Each letter has a color and a control.

00:14.411 --> 00:23.347
I start with the plain sine: the blue curve covers the gray curve,
which is the original function, for comparison.

00:24.197 --> 00:27.829
The parameter a adds a value to the whole
function.

00:27.879 --> 00:31.222
With a equal to two, the curve rises two
units.

00:31.272 --> 00:37.069
The shape does not change, only the height:
the range now goes from one to three.

00:37.919 --> 00:40.586
The b stretches the curve vertically.

00:40.636 --> 00:46.754
With b equal to three, the wave gets three
times taller, and the amplitude is three.

00:46.804 --> 00:50.499
And with a negative b, the curve turns upside
down.

00:51.349 --> 00:54.962
The c is the one that confuses students the
most.

00:55.012 --> 00:57.729
It multiplies x, inside the function.

00:57.779 --> 01:04.233
With c equal to two, the wave repeats twice as
fast, and the period drops from 2π to π.

01:04.283 --> 01:06.551
The blue band shows one period.

01:07.401 --> 01:10.765
The parameter d pushes the curve sideways.

01:10.815 --> 01:15.073
With d equal to π/2, the curve moves π/2 to
the left.

01:15.123 --> 01:20.845
Look: it became the cosine. Sine and cosine
are the same wave, shifted.

01:21.695 --> 01:25.566
In class, ask for a prediction before moving
anything.

01:25.616 --> 01:29.488
If c is three, how many waves fit between zero
and 2π?

01:29.538 --> 01:32.393
Then the student checks it on the graph.

01:32.443 --> 01:37.404
That is the simulator's question: which
parameter changes the period?

01:38.254 --> 01:43.650
This simulator came from a plain-language
request, which is right here below.

01:43.700 --> 01:49.522
Just replace what is in square brackets to
create another one, for another subject.

01:49.572 --> 01:53.059
And before taking it to the class, check the
math.
